<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Habuba Kabira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Habuba Kabira is an archaeological site on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, now flooded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-assad">Lake Assad</a> behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabqa-dam">Tabqa Dam</a>. Excavated in the late 1960s and 1970s, it revealed a planned, walled town of the late fourth millennium BCE with southern Mesopotamian architecture, pottery and administrative tools, the best-known colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> expansion.</p></div>
